/*3. Write a java program that takes an integer, n, as command line argument. Then it should create an
array of n small integer (say between 1 and 100). The task is to calculate the sum of squares of the
integers in the array. You can do this calculation in a straight-forward way using a loop. Do it, and see
how many nanoseconds it takes, for a chosen value of n. Then do the same task, by taking the first half
the array and summing up square in one thread, and summing up the squares of the rest in another
thread, and then combining the results to get the total sum. Time the second way doing the task

Start with n =100, and keep increasing its value by a factor of 10, up to 100,000,000. Write down the
times for both threaded and non-threaded calculations. With n on the x-axis and time required on the y-
axis, plot the results for threaded and non-threaded tasks. What do you observe? Can you explain this?
(you can divide nanoseconds by appropriate numbers to get milliseconds which may be easier to plot. )
*/

public class Problem3 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("Usage: java Problem3 <number_of_elements>");
            return;
        }

        int n = Integer.parseInt(args[0]);
        int[] numbers = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            numbers[i] = (int) (Math.random() * 100) + 1;
        }

        // --- Non-threaded calculation ---
        long startTime = System.nanoTime();
        SumSquares singleTask = new SumSquares(numbers, 0, n);
        singleTask.calculate();
        long nonThreadedTime = System.nanoTime() - startTime;

        // --- Threaded calculation ---
        startTime = System.nanoTime();
        SumSquares task1 = new SumSquares(numbers, 0, n / 2);
        SumSquares task2 = new SumSquares(numbers, n / 2, n);
        
        Thread t1 = new Thread(task1::calculate); 
        Thread t2 = new Thread(task2::calculate);

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        try {
            t1.join();
            t2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
        
        long totalSum = task1.getSum() + task2.getSum();
        long threadedTime = System.nanoTime() - startTime;

        // Display Results (Converting to milliseconds for readability)
        System.out.println("N = " + n);
        System.out.println("Non-threaded time: " + (nonThreadedTime / 1_000_000.0) + " ms");
        System.out.println("Threaded time:     " + (threadedTime / 1_000_000.0) + " ms");
        System.out.println("Total Sum of Squares: " + totalSum);
    }
}

class SumSquares {
    private final int[] numbers;
    private final int start, end;
    private long sum;

    public SumSquares(int[] numbers, int start, int end) {
        this.numbers = numbers;
        this.start = start;
        this.end = end;
    }

    public void calculate() {
        long localSum = 0; 
        for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
            localSum += (long) numbers[i] * numbers[i];
        }
        this.sum = localSum;
    }

    public long getSum() { 
        return sum; 
    }
}
